# Untitled

## Highly catalytic metal–organic framework coating enabled by liquid superwetting and confinement
Bo Yi\(^{1,\S}\), Yan-Lung Wong\(^{1,\S}\), Kedi Li\(^{2}\), Changshun Hou\(^{1}\), Tengrai Ma\(^{2}\), Zhengtao Xu\(^{3}\) (✉), and Xi Yao\(^{1,4}\) (✉)
© Tsinghua University Press 2022
## ABSTRACT
In this work, we report that high catalytic performance of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) can be obtained through a synergistic effect of postsynthetic modification of MOF nanoparticles and liquid superwetting and confinement in the MOF coating. Specifically, 2-ureido-4[1H]pyrimidinone (UPy) functionalized polysiloxanes were covalently appended onto the UiO-66 nanoparticles via a postsynthetic approach, which were further anchored onto different porous films through multivalent hydrogen bonding of the UPy motifs. The hydrophobic MOF coating can preserve the porosity of the solid substrates, and enable rapid liquid superwetting and confinement within the porous substrates. Using the Knoevenagel condensation as a modeled system, robust and highly catalytic performances of the MOF coating were observed on a range of aldehyde substrates. Gram-scale production of chromene, a pharmaceutical which is typically synthesized via expensive catalysis, was successfully demonstrated on the MOF coating with high yielding rates, demonstrating the great potential of the MOF coating in pharmaceutical synthesis.
## KEYWORDS
metal–organic framework, postsynthetic modification, hydrogen bonding, superwetting, liquid confinement
## 1 Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been a subject of extensive research leading to the development of a wide range of applications, including catalysis [1], separation [2], and adsorption [3]. The tunable chemistry, microporosity, and defined reaction environments make MOFs ideal candidates for catalyst design and application [4, 5]. Though the pristine MOF particles show certain catalytic activity toward various reactions, the efficacy is far from satisfactory [6–8]. Due to the tunable chemical functionality and accessible porous network of MOFs, scientists have developed postsynthetic modification as a powerful technique to engineer the surface chemistry of MOF materials [9, 10]. Different functional groups or catalytically active species have been incorporated into MOFs for enhanced catalytic activity [11, 12]. For example, organocatalysts have been decorated on MOFs to improve their catalytic capability [13–15].
Despite of the advances, designing a catalytic system based on surface functionalized MOFs needs further work and development for better overall catalytic performance. Enabling the superwetting and confinement of reactants on two-dimensional (2D) catalysts can provide a concentrated environment for prompt catalytic reaction and affording high yield [16–20]. The surface modification of hydrophobic species on MOFs can not only improve their water stability for long-term application, but also increase the wettability of hydrophobic substrates on MOFs [21–23]. Therefore, we propose that the catalytic performance of MOFs would be substantially improved with the synergy of postsynthetic modification of functional species and liquid superwetting/confinement effects on MOFs.
