Zirconium-based MOF material UiO-66-NH\(_{2}\) offers excellent thermal and chemical stability, and modifiable amino groups for postsynthetic functionalization [24]. Herein, a quadruple hydrogen-bond building block, 2-ureido-4[1H]pyrimidinone (UPy), first reported by Meijer et al. [25], has been anchored on UiO-66-NH\(_{2}\) with different hydrophobic spacers, giving a series of bifunctional MOFs. The crystallinity and morphology of resulting MOFs are retained after the modification. Remarkably, grafting the UPy-rich bifunctional species on the MOF matrix significantly improves the catalytic activity of MOFs for Knoevenagel condensation in water, which is comparable to or higher than previously reported MOF catalysts. Moreover, deposition of the MOFs onto membranes can produce MOF coatings with much improved catalytic activity to less reactive substrates, accounting to the liquid superwetting and confinement of reactants in the hydrophobic MOF coating. The applicability of the MOF coating was also illustrated under gram-scale tandem reactions and afforded the desired chromene product with 99% yield, implying its potential application in chemical synthesis and pharmaceutical industry.
