## 2.10 Deposition of \(\mathbf{PDMS@UiO-66-PDMS_{1,000}-UPy}\) on hydrophobic polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) membrane
The deposition process of Section 2.9 was repeated, and the membrane substrate was replaced with the PTFE membrane (Whatman TE36, diameter of \(47\,\mathrm{mm}\), pore size of \(0.45\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\)).
2.11 Typical procedure for the liquid-confined Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehydes and ethyl cyanoacetate
The Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehyde and ethyl cyanoacetate was used as a model reaction. A droplet \((12.5\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) of DMSO- \(d_{6}\) solutions of benzaldehyde \((0.025\,\mathrm{mmol})\) and a droplet \((12.5\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) of DMSO- \(d_{6}\) solutions of ethyl cyanoacetate \((0.0375\,\mathrm{mmol})\) were added onto the surface of a piece of MOF coating (dimension of \(1\,\mathrm{cm}\times1\,\mathrm{cm}\), placed on a glass plate) using a \(0.2\,\mathrm{mL}\) autopipette. The setup was covered by a lip and left for an hour. Afterwards, the MOF coating and the glass plate were washed by DMSO- \(d_{6}\) \((0.2\,\mathrm{mL}\times3)\) to extract the desired product and any remaining substrates. The DMSO- \(d_{6}\) portions were then combined and analyzed by solution \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR to determine the yield and selectivity. For substituted benzaldehydes, the procedure was the same.
2.12 General procedures for the gram-scale production of 2-amino-4-(4-bromophenyl)-5,6,7,8-tetrahydro-7,7- dimethyl-5-oxo-4H-chromene-3-carbonitrile by PDMS@ UiO-66-PDMS1,000-UPy coated membrane substrates
The PDMS@UiO-66-PDMS1,000-UPy coated membrane substrates (diameter of \(47\,\mathrm{mm}\), \(40\,\mathrm{mg}\) loading of UiO-66-PDMS1,000-UPy for each coated membrane) was carefully placed in a \(25\,\mathrm{mL}\) clear glass vial (the back of the membrane was vertically contacted with the inner wall of the glass vial). Malononitrile \((0.337\,\mathrm{g},5.1\,\mathrm{mmol})\), 4-bromobenzaldehyde \((0.629\,\mathrm{g},3.4\,\mathrm{mmol})\), dimedone \((0.477\,\mathrm{g},3.4\,\mathrm{mmol})\), and DI water \((14\,\mathrm{mL})\) were added. The vial was screw-capped and the mixture was stirred at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{h}\). After cooling to room temperature, the resulting solid was collected by suction filtration, washed by DI water \((100\,\mathrm{mL}\times3)\), and then dissolved in MeOH \((200\,\mathrm{mL})\) in order to extract the desired product. Removal of organic phase afforded a crude product which was purified by flash column chromatography using DCM as the eluent to give a white solid as the purified product (the corresponding yield and selectivity were calculated based on dimedone and summarized in the Table S6 in the ESM). \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR \((400\,\mathrm{MHz}\), DMSO- \(d_{6}\)): \(\delta=7.48\) (d, \(J=8.4\,\mathrm{Hz}\), 1H), \(7.35\) (d, \(J=8.4\,\mathrm{Hz}\), 1H), \(7.16\) (d, \(J=8.4\,\mathrm{Hz}\), 1H), \(7.11\) (d, \(J=8.4\,\mathrm{Hz}\), 1H), \(7.08\) (d, 2H), \(4.18\) (d, \(J=6.1\,\mathrm{Hz}\), 1H, 4-Br- \(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{-CH}_{2}\)), \(2.25\) (d, \(J=16.1\,\mathrm{Hz}\), 1H, \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O})\)- \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\)), \(2.10\) (d, \(J=16.0\,\mathrm{Hz}\), 1H, \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O})\mathrm{-CH}_{2}\)), \(1.03\) (s, 3H, \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\)), and \(0.94\) (s, 3H, \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\)).
## 2.13 General characterization
The \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectra were recorded on a Bruker DRX 500 NMR spectrometer. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained by a scanning electron microscope (FEI Quanta 450 FEG). For the measurements, the samples were placed on carbon tapes and coated with a layer of platinum. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were observed on Philips/FEI Tecnai
12 transmission electron microscope. Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were recorded by an IR spectrometer (PerkinElmer Spectrum 100) with \(2\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) of resolution. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) results were recorded by a Bruker D2 Phaser diffractometer. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed on a TA Q600 differential thermal analyzer. Contact angles were measured on an OCA 20 system (Data Physics). The porosity and surface area analyses were performed using a Quantachrome Autosorb iQ gas sorption analyzer.
## 3 Results and discussion
To synthesize the UPy-functionalized UiO-66, isocyanate group was first installed onto UiO-66-NH2 by the condensation of amino groups with isocyanate groups of HDI. The obtained sample was named as UiO-66-HDI, which features a distinct IR stretch at \(2,269\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and thus reveals the presence of isocyanate groups (Fig. S1 in the ESM). The extent of isocyanato functionalization was approximate \(25\%\) as calculated from the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectra (Fig. S2 in the ESM). PDMS is a kind of hydrophobic polymer with great thermal and chemical stability, flexibility, and wide applicability [28–31]. Employing PDMS as a hydrophobic spacer, the UPy-PDMS1,000 segment \((\mathrm{PDMS}_{1,000}\) refers to the molecular weight of PDMS chain) was then incorporated and gave rise to UiO-66-PDMS1,000-UPy (Fig. S3 in the ESM). The disappearance of the stretching vibration of isocyanate group in the FT-IR spectrum indicates the successful reaction (Fig. S4 in the ESM).
Different spacers between UPy and UiO-66 have been synthesized (Fig. 1). Specifically, UiO-66-UPy with \(n\)-hexyl spacer, UiO-66-Pri-UPy with priamine spacer, and UiO-66-PDMS3,000-UPy with longer PDMS chain were prepared (Figs. S4–S6 in the ESM). The degrees of modification have been calculated from the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectra (Fig. S7 in the ESM) and summarized in Table S1 in the ESM. SEM images displayed the morphology of the octahedral crystallites of UiO-66-NH2 and modified MOFs with particle size of several hundreds of nanometers (Fig. S8 in the ESM). TEM images illustrate that the outer surface of MOF particles is surrounded by polymeric constituents (Fig. S9 in the ESM). The structural integrity of modified MOFs was confirmed by PXRD (Fig. S10 in the ESM). The thermal stability of functionalized MOFs was examined by TGA. As shown in Fig. S11 in the ESM, the modified MOFs generally exhibited a good thermal stability. The weight loss at around \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) can be attributed to the thermal cleavage of urea linkage in UPy-modified MOFs, as commonly observed in urea functionalized MOFs [32]. In addition, the obvious difference in the initial weight loss observed in the TGA curves should be due to the more hydrophilic nature of UiO-66-NH2 that could contain higher water content than functionalized hydrophobic MOFs (Fig. S12 in the ESM).
As rich urea offered by UPy motifs, we envision that the integration of UPy-based supramolecular polymers into MOF matrix will impart catalytic activity to UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). To verify this, the pristine and functionalized MOF materials were used as heterogeneous catalysts in Knoevenagel condensation, which is an important carbon–carbon bond formation method for fine chemicals and pharmaceuticals [34, 35]. Since Knoevenagel condensation is a well-studied catalytic model using base catalysts [36, 37], the catalytic mechanism is not the strength in this study. In detail, the catalytic reactions were performed by mixing benzaldehyde, malononitrile, and the MOF catalyst in water at room temperature (RT). As summarized in Table 1, the control experiments resulted in \(42\%\) yield for the blank, \(56\%\) yield for UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), and \(72\%\) yield for the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{PDMS}_{1,000}\mathrm{-UPy}\). The grafting of UPy onto the UiO-66 surface significantly increases the product yield (up to \(93\%\)), displaying that the UPy motifs can serve as another catalytically active site for the substrates except for the amino groups on the catalyst. The increased yield could imply that the UPy motif shows higher activity than amino groups. Moreover, the insertion of priamine or PDMS segments between the MOF matrix and UPy units can further increase the product yield and turn over frequency (TOF) of the catalysts. The kinetic studies of Knoevenagel condensation between benzaldehyde with malononitrile in water with and without UiO-66-PDMS1,000-UPy were also conducted and the results are shown in Fig. S13 in the ESM. The conversion of the reactants without MOF catalyst
increased slowly with time. When UiO-66-PDMS1,000-UPy solid was added, the reaction proceeded much faster and achieved \(100\%\) conversion within \(60\,\mathrm{min}\).
The basicity of these catalysts has been determined by the Hammett indicator and benzoic acid titration method [27]. As shown in Table S2 in the ESM, the basicity of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) increases after modification with UPy motifs, which should contribute to the growth of catalytic activity of the MOF catalyst. Moreover, the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area and pore property of the MOF catalysts have been calculated by the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption isotherm (Fig. S14 in the ESM). Table S3 in the ESM summarizes the BET surface areas and pore volumes of the pristine and UPy-polymer modified MOFs. After surface functionalization of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) with UPy motifs or UPy-polymers, its specific surface area and pore volume can be largely reserved, especially for UiO-66-PDMS1,000-UPy, around one half retained. These results confirm the exposure and accessibility of the active sites in the modified MOF catalysts. Table S4 in the ESM compares the catalytic efficiency of UiO-66-PDMS1,000-UPy with previous studies in Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehyde and malononitrile, which shows that the UPy-PDMS functionalized UiO-66 demonstrates the optimum overall performance.
Substituted benzaldehydes were also tested to expand the scope of substrates. Each of these aldehydes showed excellent yields (over \(94\%\), Table S5 in the ESM). The reusability of the UiO-66-
\(\mathrm{PDMS}_{1,000}\)-UPy catalyst is highlighted in Fig. 2. Specifically, excellent yields were obtained throughout seven consecutive catalytic cycles (Fig. 2(a)). The crystallinity, hydrophobicity, and morphology of the UPy functionalized MOF catalyst remained almost intact after the seven catalytic cycles (Figs. 2(b)–2(d)), further establishing the UiO-66-PDMS\(_{1,000}\)-UPy as a robust and reusable heterogeneous catalyst. In addition, the FT-IR spectra of the catalyst also show that the chemical features of UiO-66-PDMS\(_{1,000}\)-UPy before and after the catalytic cycles remain almost the same (Fig. S15 in the ESM). The mass of catalyst would not decrease obviously during the catalytic cycles (Fig. S16 in the ESM).
Due to the multiple hydrogen bonding sites on UPy motifs, the MOF catalysts can be fabricated as a coating with good mechanical stability. To prepare the catalytically active MOF coating, UiO-66-PDMS\(_{1,000}\)-UPy was mixed with a previously reported catechol-terminated polysiloxane-urea polymer (PDMS-Cat) [26] and gave rise to a MOF/polymer composite termed PDMS@UiO-66-PDMS\(_{1,000}\)-UPy (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). MOF coating can be obtained by depositing PDMS@UiO-66-PDMS\(_{1,000}\)-UPy on diverse supports, such as cellulose and PTFE membranes. The PDMS@UiO-66-PDMS\(_{1,000}\)-UPy composite shows high substrate binding ability due to the multiple non-covalent interactions between the catechol/UPy groups and the membrane substrates (Figs. 3(c) and 3(d)) [28, 38]. The mechanical robustness of the MOF coating has been verified by tape peeling test (Fig. S17 in the ESM). The membrane supported MOF coating shows homogeneous distribution of UiO-66-PDMS\(_{1,000}\)-UPy, as confirmed by the SEM images and photographs (Figs. 3(e)–3(l) and Fig. S18 in the ESM). Moreover, the substrate membranes show hydrophobicity after the deposition of MOF catalysts (Fig. S19 in the ESM).
The catalytic efficacy of the MOF coating was investigated by selecting ethyl cyanoacetate as the substrate to proceed Knoevenagel condensation with aldehydes (Table 2), where ethyl cyanoacetate is less reactive than malononitrile. The control experiments reveal that the pristine UiO-66-PDMS\(_{1,000}\)-UPy powder or PDMS@UiO-66-NH\(_{2}\) coated cellulose membrane did not show catalytic effect to the reaction between benzaldehyde and ethyl cyanoacetate, due to the ultralow reactivity of ethyl cyanoacetate. In contrast, the MOF coating shows highly improved catalytic performance with the reaction yield increased to 75%. Various aldehydes, such as bromobenzaldehyde and nitrobenzaldehyde, have been used to demonstrate the great catalytic activity of the MOF coating. This highly increased catalytic efficacy can be ascribed to the liquid superwetting and confinement effect of the catalyst with better substrate enriching and reaction microenvironment, as depicted in Table 2 [16, 18]. When the reactant solution spreads on the MOF coating, the high affinity of the hydrophobic MOFs would facilitate the superwetting of the organic reactant on the MOF coating. Subsequently, the nano/micro-pores among the MOF particles could provide a confined effect to the reactant for enriched substrate and thus improved catalytic efficiency.
To illustrate the applicability of the MOF coating, a one-pot three-component, gram-scale catalytic reaction for production of 2-amino-4H-chromene was performed (Table S6 in the ESM). The chromene scaffolds are vital structural motifs in a wide number of natural products and synthetic drugs, which possess a broad range of pharmacological activities such as antitumor, anti-inflammatory, anticoagulant, and antitubercular [39]. In the gram-scale experiment, 4-bromobenzaldehyde, dimedone,
